REFERENCE — Nodes.pm (pve-mac-monitor v2.3.0)
Files/sections: /usr/share/perl5/PVE/API2/Nodes.pm

1) Status fields — status method, immediately before `return $res;`
   Anchor: `name => 'status'`, then `$res->{rootfs}`, then the next
   `return $res;` (whitespace-tolerant, tabs or spaces).

   # >>> pve-mac-monitor BEGIN (v2.3.0, idempotent)
   eval {
       my $mac_state_file = '/var/lib/pve-mac-monitor/state.json';
       if (-f $mac_state_file && -r _) {
           require JSON;
           my $mac_raw = PVE::Tools::file_get_contents($mac_state_file);
           my $mac_data = JSON::decode_json($mac_raw);
           if (ref($mac_data) eq 'HASH') {
               $res->{macstats} = $mac_data;
               $res->{mac} = {
                   used  => ($mac_data->{current} // 0) + 0,
                   total => ($mac_data->{limit}   // 32) + 0,
               };
               $res->{mac_current} = ($mac_data->{current} // 0) + 0;
               $res->{mac_limit}   = ($mac_data->{limit}   // 32) + 0;
           }
       }
   };
   # <<< pve-mac-monitor END

   - `mac` is {used,total}, the same record shape as stock `cpu`/`maxcpu`
     rows, so the native bar and renderer work unchanged.
   - `macstats` carries the full state (peaks, timestamps) for the text row.
   - eval-guarded: missing/corrupt state never breaks the node status API.

2) History endpoint — new register_method, inserted before `netstat`
   (fallback: end of file). Anchor: `__PACKAGE__->register_method({` +
   `name => 'netstat'`.

   # >>> pve-mac-monitor history BEGIN (v2.3.0, idempotent)
   __PACKAGE__->register_method({
       name => 'mac_history',
       path => 'mac-history',
       method => 'GET',
       permissions => { check => ['perm', '/nodes/{node}', ['Sys.Audit']] },
       proxyto => 'node',
       parameters => { ... node,
                       timeframe (hour|day|week|month|year, default hour),
                       cf (average|max, default average) ... },
       returns => { type => "array" },
       code => sub {
           my ($param) = @_;
           my %win = (hour => 3700, day => 90000, week => 700000,
                      month => 3100000, year => 32000000);
           my $tf = $param->{timeframe} // 'hour';
           my $cf = $param->{cf} // 'average';
           my $cut = time() - ($win{$tf} // $win{hour});
           # read /var/lib/pve-mac-monitor/history.json uncapped
           # [{t,v}, ...] -> [{time,mac}, ...]
           # window by timeframe, consolidate to <=800 points
           # (average, or max when cf=max, mirroring rrddata)
       }});
   # <<< pve-mac-monitor history END

   - Mirrors rrddata behaviour: numerics only, windowed, <=800 points, so the
     stock proxmoxRRDChart renders it like CPU/Memory/Server Load.
   - Uncapped read is deliberate: PVE::Tools::file_get_contents caps at 1MB
     and would silently return [] for long-retention rings.
   - Sys.Audit matches the node status permission level.

